## Summary

The speaker, a sports medicine physician and endocrinologist who co-founded a women's health and sports performance institute, argues that research on female athletes is thin and often poorly built. She gives the history of excluding women from research, the health burdens female athletes face, and the results of literature audits that score studies on population, athlete caliber, menstrual status method, journal and sample size. She warns about unvalidated wearables, weak hormone measurement, survey bias and AI repeating errors, and offers a workflow for validating wearable data. Her answer to closing the gap is teamwork across clinicians, athletes and data analysts.

## Key claims

- Women's sport is growing: Title IX, women now have 44% of sport opportunities, more than 160 female sports leagues exist, and fandom has risen, but gaps remain in media investment and prize money. [03:06] [03:35] [04:07] [06:40]
- Female athletes face higher rates of concussion, ACL tears and bone stress injuries, and relative energy deficiency in sport is very prevalent, partly through inadvertent under-fueling. [06:40] [07:10]
- Team USA athletes asked for research on pregnancy and postpartum, menstrual cycle symptoms, birth control, fueling across the cycle, mental health, recovery and more female coaches. [07:42] [08:12] [08:43]
- It is not known when sex differences change training, because they are not studied enough or studied well. [08:43] [09:14]
- Women of childbearing potential were excluded from early drug trials from 1977 until the early 1990s; drugs in common use have different pharmacokinetics and adverse reactions in women. [09:14] [10:16] [10:46]
- Women's health gets a small share of private health investment despite an estimated return on investment, and research funding does not match burden of disease (obstetric complications affect 33% of birthing women worldwide, yet only 2% of US registered trials address obstetrics). [11:48] [12:20] [12:51]
- Sports medicine textbooks historically treated female athletes as a "special population," and the speaker refused to write such a chapter, asking instead for each chapter to say what is known and unknown in women. [13:21] [13:51]
- Only 6% of articles in top sports medicine and science journals from 2014 to 2020 focused solely on women. [14:21]
- Her group audits the literature on population, athlete caliber (tiers 0 to 5), menstrual status method, research theme, journal impact and sample size. [14:53] [15:23] [15:54] [16:25] [16:56]
- Asking about menstrual status is not the same as testing hormones, and a withdrawal bleed on a birth control pill is not a natural cycle. [16:25]
- Audit results: 99.5% of supplement research did not categorize or standardize menstrual cycle; female participation rose from 39% to 44% between audit periods while female-only studies did not improve. [16:56] [18:30] [19:00]
- The Women's Health Initiative hormone trial was stopped in 2002 after harms, and a 2016 reanalysis found the problem was women who started therapy after age 60 or more than 10 years after menopause, which left a generation of women without treatment. [21:03] [22:06] [22:36] [23:07]
- Surveys are cheap and good for prevalence and early ideas but cross-sectional, with selection, recall and non-response bias. [27:41] [28:12]
- Wearables vary in what they measure and their validity, and they need testing against a gold standard; they also raise equity, algorithm bias and privacy issues. [28:44] [29:14] [29:44] [30:16]
- AI can repeat and reinforce errors in numbers and citations while sounding more confident. [30:46] [31:17]
- Hormone testing methods each have limits: saliva needs many samples and has weaker assays, blood is precise but time-sensitive, dried blood spot needs repeated sampling, and urine is well validated against serum but suited to profiling patterns. [31:47] [32:17] [32:48]
- Her research approach is audit, observational studies, intervention studies, repeat, then advocacy and education, because it takes about 17 years from discovery to common practice. [27:10]
- The answer to the data gap is teamwork among endocrinologists, sports physicians, athletes and data analysts. [34:53] [35:24]

## Numbers and definitions

- Title IX in 1972: more than 3 million more high school girls and 200,000 collegiate women playing each year; 44% of sport opportunities now go to women. [03:06] [03:35]
- More than 160 female sports leagues; 57% increase in fandom over 5 years (McKinsey survey, caption spelling "McKenzie"). [03:35] [04:07]
- 9% of sports media investment goes to women's sports; the Women's World Cup prize pool is a quarter of the men's. [06:40]
- Concussion 1.4 times; ACL tears 2 to 8 times; bone stress injuries 3 times (women versus men). [06:40] [07:10]
- 1977 exclusion; 1990 NIH Office of Research on Women's Health; 1993 reversal; 1994 FDA Office of Women's Health; 1998 demographic rule; 2002 pregnancy registries guidance; 2016 NIH both-sexes requirement; 2024 White House initiative. [09:14] to [11:48]
- 6% of private healthcare investment goes to women's health; estimated three-time return. [11:48]
- 33% versus 2%: obstetric complications versus trials addressing obstetrics. [12:20] [12:51]
- 6% of articles solely on women, top journals, 2014 to 2020. [14:21]
- Athlete caliber: tier 0 sedentary, tier 1 moving a little, tier 2 working out at least three times a week with intent to compete at least once a year, up to tier 5 world class. [15:23] [15:54]
- 99.5% of supplement studies not standardizing menstrual cycle. [16:56]
- Audits (2011 to 2013 versus 2021 to 2023): 39% to 44% with female participants; female-only 8.76 to 7.15 (unit not stated); male-only about 28% to about 19%; menstrual status reporting from none to about 5.6%; 7% silver and 0.5% gold classification. [18:30] [19:00] [19:30]
- Women's Health Initiative: 161,000 women, about 70,000 in the randomized trial. [21:03] [21:35]
- $220 million initiative and $50 million institute funding; 15,000 square feet. [23:37] [25:40]
- 17 years from discovery to common practice. [27:10]
- Possible caption garbles: "Kate Ari" and "Dr. Arian," "Elena Myers Taylor," "Cali Humphre," "Michaela Shiffren," "Claroo" and "Clara Woo," "Joseph Sai," "Satchin," "Dave and Jane Aught," thalidomide spellings, and "gender parody." [00:00] [02:04] [06:09] [09:14] [23:37] [25:40]

## Methods and frameworks named

- Standardized literature audit: topic search, screening, then population, athlete caliber, menstrual status, research theme, journal impact and sample size. [14:53] to [16:56]
- Gold, silver and bronze classification of menstrual status reporting. [19:00]
- Research pipeline: audit, observational and prevalence studies, intervention studies, replication, advocacy and education. [27:10]
- Wearable validation workflow: check data fit and question impact, prepare data (sensor accuracy, missing data, outliers), validate against a trusted reference, reproduce known values or trends, analyze, then share only if conclusions hold. [33:19] [33:51] [34:22]

## Limits and open questions

- Much of the talk is context and history, not data analysis; the audit figures are from her group's work, and sources for several statistics (ratios, investment share, 17 years) are not named.
- The female-only figures lack units and the audit topic coverage is not stated. [18:30]
- Injury risk ratios are given without ranges or sources. [06:40] [07:10]
- She says we do not know when cycle effects matter in training; no training recommendation is offered. [08:43]
- The speaker leads an institute that receives philanthropic funding, and her recommendations (teamwork, more research) are institutional views. [25:40]
- The political remark about the White House initiative is her opinion. [11:48]
- No Q&A: the session ran out of time. [35:55]

## Q&A

No Q&A; the host said there was no time and invited attendees to meet the speaker outside. [35:55]
